&lt;p&gt;  The single photon emission computed tomograph (SPECT) acquires information on the concentration of radionuclides introduced into the patient&#39;s body. It is useful in the diagnosis of several clinical conditions including:&lt;&sol;p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;    
&lt;li&gt;    stress fracture.&lt;&sol;li&gt;

&lt;li&gt;    spondylosis.&lt;&sol;li&gt;

&lt;li&gt;    infection (e.g., discitis).&lt;&sol;li&gt;

&lt;li&gt;    tumor (e.g., osteoid osteoma).&lt;&sol;li&gt;

&lt;li&gt;    analyze blood flow to an organ, as in the case of myocardial viability.&lt;&sol;li&gt;

&lt;li&gt;    differentiate ischemic heart disease from dilated cardiomyopathy.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
